<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ijaw people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ijo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ijaw are a people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, living mostly in Bayelsa, Rivers and Delta states and numbering several million. Fishermen and traders of the creeks and mangrove swamps, they founded city-states such as Nembe, Bonny, Kalabari and Okrika, which became major centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> and later the palm-oil trade. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> is now central to Nigeria's oil industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ijaw-people.jpg" alt="Ijaw people" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quinn Dombrowski from Chicago, USA · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ijaw_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
